IN THE COURT OF SPECIAL SESSIONS JUDGE (ECONOMIC OFFENCES), UDAIPUR
Sessions Case No. _____ of 2025

State of Rajasthan
VERSUS
Rajesh Kumar Gupta

FIR No.: 456/2025, P.S. Economic Offences Wing, Udaipur
U/Sections: 420, 406 BNS, 2023

APPLICATION UNDER SECTION 250 BHARATIYA NAGARIK SURAKSHA SANHITA, 2023
(READ WITH SECTION 227 CrPC) FOR DISCHARGE OF THE ACCUSED

MOST RESPECTFULLY SHOWETH:

1. That the Chargesheet and accompanying material on record, even if taken on face value without rebuttal, fail to disclose the essential statutory ingredients of cheating (Section 318 BNS / 420 IPC) or criminal breach of trust (Section 316 BNS / 406 IPC).

2. That the foundational test of cheating as settled by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Hridaya Ranjan Pd. Verma v. State of Bihar (2000) 4 SCC 168 mandates that the accused must have harbored fraudulent or dishonest intention at the very time of the inducement. The record demonstrates that for over 4 months, profits were regularly paid and credited directly to investor accounts.

3. That the distinction between civil liability for breach of commercial promise and criminal cheating has been reiterated in Vesa Holdings (P) Ltd. v. State of Kerala (2015) 8 SCC 293. The failure of business ventures due to adverse market trends cannot be criminalized through an omnibus FIR.

4. That Section 406 BNS requires entrustment with dominion over property. Here, money was received under an equity participation contract with risk-sharing provisions, not in trust or fiduciary capacity.

5. That continuing the criminal trial upon these admitted civil agreements constitutes an abuse of the judicial process.

PRAYER:
Wherefore, it is most respectfully prayed that this Hon'ble Court may graciously be pleased to discharge the Accused Rajesh Kumar Gupta of the offences alleged under Sections 420 and 406 BNS.

Advocate for Accused
Dated: 25.07.2025, Udaipur
